    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Investigate layout only as an incremental improvement to threaded SIMD. Prototype reusable x/y/z planes allocated once, initialized from Body records and refreshed during position integration, avoiding per-force allocation and packing. Preserve phase barriers and provide allocation-failure fallback. Compare against AoS with identical arithmetic and scheduling, including all conversion costs. The nearly identical existing A timings do not justify retaining packing by itself.",
        "B": "Use 54bd03f810e143a0 as the SIMD donor. Package its four-target NEON computation for independent target-block execution; retain scalar tails for arbitrary n and the non-NEON fallback. Preserve every target’s ascending-j accumulation, all interactions, double sqrt/division before float conversion, expression ordering, and existing contraction behavior. Prefer four targets over the measured slower eight-target version. Do not introduce reciprocal approximations, sqrtf, or horizontal force reductions.",
        "C": "Use 60e063cb2c7546e6 as the threading foundation: one persistent OpenMP team, static target ownership, and barriers separating force computation, position integration, and the next timestep. Adapt ownership to four-target blocks without nested parallelism or duplicated scalar tails. Retain the small-input serialized path initially and respect the existing four-core runtime limits. Keep full-Body ping-pong fusion secondary: its measured 0.174073 s did not beat the simpler two-phase implementation.",
        "G": "Anchor integration on 60e063cb2c7546e6 and first combine it only with B’s four-target NEON kernel. Workshare complete target blocks, assign remainder bodies exactly once, and retain both timestep phase barriers. Measure this complete candidate against the unchanged 0.169682 s C control before adding anything else. Then consider A’s reusable layout as a separate measured increment; do not automatically stack packing, eight-target unrolling, or double buffering. Use only the existing correctness checks, compiler, target, and measurement budget. Preserve ten timesteps, constants, reference floating-point behavior, native int-count-prefixed input, and headerless Body-record output. Select the fastest admissible measured whole program; retain C alone if integration regresses.",
        "analysis": "Only Problem C and src/nbody.c are relevant. Baseline time is 0.655285 s. Persistent-team OpenMP reaches 0.169682 s (~3.86x), four-target NEON 0.323847 s (~2.02x), and scalar tiling/layout variants about 0.597 s (~1.10x). Eight-target NEON and full-Body ping-pong are observed slower than their simpler alternatives; packed and unpacked scalar tiling are effectively tied in these measurements. The strongest untested synthesis is four-target SIMD across independently threaded target blocks. Its gains must be measured, not inferred by multiplying standalone speedups. Preserve the exact all-pairs computation and per-target accumulation order."
      },
